Hong Kong: From an Industrialized City
to a Center of Manufacturing-Related Services1
Zhigang Tao2
YC Richard Wong3
March 2002
Abstract
Significant transformation of economic activities has taken place in Hong Kong in the past two decades. Hong Kong’s manufacturing industry has declined substantially relative to its service industry, in terms of employment and of contribution to GDP. Hong Kong has emerged as a center of services, mainly manufacturing-related producer services. While growth of producer services is expected for most advanced economies, Hong Kong's transformation from an industrialized city to a center of manufacturing-related services has been dramatically sped up by the opening up of the mainland Chinese economy in the past two decades. In addition to its relocation of manufacturing to mainland China, Hong Kong has played an increasingly important role as an intermediary for trade between mainland China and the world market.
